WEBVTT

00:00:00.350 --> 00:00:03.347
Every day, the world throws away about a billion meals.

00:00:03.347 --> 00:00:06.670
Forty percent of that waste comes from restaurants and shops.

00:00:07.370 --> 00:00:09.564
Surplus food apps sell you a mystery bag.

00:00:09.564 --> 00:00:13.470
LastCall shows every item before you pay, checked against your allergies.

00:00:14.170 --> 00:00:15.588
Just say who you're feeding.

00:00:15.588 --> 00:00:20.450
An AI agent searches every shop's real leftovers, and you pay with PayPal, straight to the shop.

00:00:21.450 --> 00:00:26.400
And small shops earn from food they'd throw away, with AI teammates that help them sell out before close.

00:00:30.656 --> 00:00:31.928
Arjun runs Saffron Table.

00:00:31.928 --> 00:00:36.766
He set it up in minutes, and LastCall verified his PayPal account with PayPal before saving it.

00:00:38.586 --> 00:00:42.326
Every dish has a photo, his own price, and the allergens he verified.

00:00:50.502 --> 00:00:52.824
Maya's partner has a severe nut allergy.

00:00:52.824 --> 00:00:54.972
She asks for dinner for two, tonight.

00:00:55.405 --> 00:01:00.381
Ask LastCall, the shopping agent, checks every open shop with the store's own tools:

00:01:00.381 --> 00:01:03.165
live stock, prices, pickup times and allergens.

00:01:03.540 --> 00:01:05.552
Her allergy is a hard rule, in code:

00:01:05.552 --> 00:01:09.410
only items the shop verified nut free, and it shows what it left out.

00:01:11.433 --> 00:01:13.933
Saffron Table's dinner fits, at half price.

00:01:15.001 --> 00:01:17.571
Then a walk-in buys Arjun's last samosas.

00:01:18.927 --> 00:01:23.127
Within a second, her bundle offers a swap, and she can't pay until she approves it.

00:01:24.185 --> 00:01:26.725
Holding it reserves the food, so nothing sells twice.

00:01:28.966 --> 00:01:30.840
Now, let's break something on purpose:

00:01:30.840 --> 00:01:32.466
PayPal's next reply will be lost.

00:01:33.433 --> 00:01:35.195
Maya pays in PayPal's own window.

00:01:35.195 --> 00:01:40.053
LastCall builds the order on its server, at its own prices, paid straight to Saffron Table.

00:01:40.433 --> 00:01:43.764
PayPal takes the payment, but the reply never arrives.

00:01:43.764 --> 00:01:48.083
LastCall asks PayPal, finds the capture, and confirms the order, once.

00:01:50.366 --> 00:01:52.316
Maya asks if she was charged twice.

00:01:52.866 --> 00:01:56.705
The support agent looks the payment up with PayPal before drafting a reply.

00:01:56.705 --> 00:01:58.086
Arjun checks it, and sends.

00:01:59.700 --> 00:02:02.230
He marks the order ready, and her pass updates live.

00:02:02.698 --> 00:02:05.278
At pickup, he checks her code and hands the food over.

00:02:07.033 --> 00:02:11.413
When someone can't come, a manager refunds in one step, through the shop's own PayPal account.

00:02:17.333 --> 00:02:24.613
Before close, the close-out agent drafts a plan for what's left, checked in code against the shop's price floor and live stock.

00:02:30.067 --> 00:02:31.953
Four agents work for every shop.

00:02:31.953 --> 00:02:36.197
They use tools, but can't change a price or move money without a person.

00:02:39.134 --> 00:02:43.274
Each shop sees what it recovered, and what to make less of tomorrow.

00:02:44.866 --> 00:02:49.071
LastCall keeps ten percent of each order, billed weekly with a PayPal invoice.

00:02:49.071 --> 00:02:50.526
Shoppers pay nothing extra.

00:02:51.633 --> 00:02:58.083
And every PayPal webhook is verified and de-duplicated, and every agent step and payment is traced.

00:03:00.983 --> 00:03:07.343
LastCall. Less food in the bin, safer meals for people with allergies, and new revenue for small shops.

